+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`session_hook.py` delivers the session-end signal from `_end_impl` through the single generic `request(...)` helper, which merges a caller-supplied headers dict over `Authorization`/`Content-Type`. The end call site (the only place that POSTs to `/v1/executions/{executionId}/signal`) already passes a literal three-entry headers dict; `session` (the validated host session id) is in scope at that point. The Runtime side already reads `x-graphhelm-actor-session` case-insensitively and validates it (`apps/cli/src/commands/serve/mod.rs`, "declared_session" block). See proposal.md for motivation.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- One logical edit at the existing end-request call site so the `agent_session_ended` POST carries `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` with the validated host session id.
+- Extend the existing end-request test to pin that behavior, without weakening or restructuring the suite.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- No change to the Runtime (Rust) actor parsing, signal schema, state file format, idempotency keys, or retry semantics.
+- No new headers on SessionStart/briefing requests. No new header-building helper function.
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. **Add the entry to the literal headers dict at the `_end_impl` call site**, e.g. `"X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session": session` alongside `X-GraphHelm-Actor`, `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type`, `Idempotency-Key`. Alternative considered: a shared helper building all four attribution headers — rejected per the request's explicit constraint and because there is exactly one call site that needs it; a second consumer (there is none today) can factor it out.
+2. **Value source is the local `session` variable** returned by the hook's identity check (`_session(payload)`), which has already enforced the `ID` shape and equality with `GRAPHHELM_SESSION_ID` when that binding is set. Alternative considered: re-reading the payload at the call site — redundant, since `_end_impl` runs only after validation passes.
+3. **Header-only change; nothing persisted.** The session header rides the HTTP request like the other attribution headers; it is not written into the hook's local state file or the signal body. This keeps retry bytes deterministic (retries rebuild the same headers from the same validated inputs) and leaves stored-signal validation untouched.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- **[Test key spelling]** Python's `urllib.request.Request.add_header` capitalizes header names, so the wire form of this header arrives as `X-graphhelm-actor-session` in the in-process test server's captured `dict(self.headers)` — the same reason the existing suite asserts `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Type`, not `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type`. The new assertion MUST use the capitalized wire form; a test asserting the canonical `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` spelling would pass trivially against a missing header via `.get()` or fail spuriously against a present one. Mitigation: copy the spelling pattern from the adjacent existing assertion, and verify the whole end suite green. *Recorded at apply (2026-10-26): an in-process probe driving the real `request()` path through the same in-process server captured the wire key as `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session` (title-cased per segment), which matches the adjacent `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Type` assertion; the lowercase `X-graphhelm-actor-session` shown above does not arrive. The new assertion therefore uses `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session`.*
+- **[Case-insensitive consumers]** Any consumer that compares header names case-sensitively could miss the value. Observed consumers do not: the Rust Runtime looks up `x-graphhelm-actor-session` (http server lookup is case-insensitive), matching how every other `X-GraphHelm-*` header already travels. No action needed; recorded so a future case-sensitive reader doesn't treat the wire casing as the contract.
+- **[Behavioral surface]** The start/briefing path shares `request(...)`; editing only the end call site's dict leaves it untouched, which the extended suite (start tests asserting exact captured header dicts/Authorization values) continues to observe.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Single commit touching two files; no data migration. Rollback = revert the commit; old headers are a strict subset of the new ones and the Runtime accepts both (the header is optional there), so mixed-version Runtime/hook pairs are safe during rollout.
